Title:
No Agent, No Problem: Discovering Remote EDR

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- This post shows how to build “agentless” remote monitoring on Windows by abusing the Performance Logs and Alerts (PLA) COM/DCOM APIs (pla.dll) to enumerate, create, and manage ETW-based data collector sets on remote hosts.  
- It explains how logman/PerfMon interact with PLA interfaces and how namespaces (e.g., Session vs Service vs AutoSession) affect visibility—enabling trace sessions that default tooling may not list.  
- The author demonstrates remote enumeration via `IDataCollectorSetCollection::GetDataCollectorSets`, and remote creation/editing via `IDataCollectorSet` + `IDataCollectorCollection::CreateDataCollector` / `CreateDataCollectorFromXml`, finalized with `Commit()` and `Start()`.  
- Authentication/privilege requirements are discussed (local admin typically required), along with running collectors as SYSTEM or configured credentials and writing ETL output to local or remote paths.  
- A PoC tool (JonMon-Lite) is presented to remotely stand up ETW collection on multiple machines, pull ETL data back, and parse/forward events into local Event Viewer for near-real-time analysis.  
- It’s useful for defenders wanting lightweight visibility on systems without EDR agents, and for red teams as a stealthy recon/telemetry mechanism and as a way to tamper with/disable ETW sessions.

Technical Focus:
- PLA (pla.dll) COM/DCOM interfaces: IDataCollectorSet, IDataCollectorSetCollection, IDataCollectorCollection
- Remote ETW trace session management via logman/PLA (enumerate/create/modify/stop)
- Data collector namespaces (Session, Service, AutoSession, etc.) and “hidden” collectors
- ETW provider collection and ETL output handling (flush timers, local vs remote storage)
- Credentialing and execution context (SYSTEM vs SetCredentials, SMB/share writes)

Use Cases:
- Remote, agentless ETW telemetry collection from servers/endpoints where EDR deployment is undesirable
- Enumerating remote ETW sessions/providers and auditing what telemetry is being collected
- Building distributed detection pipelines (ETW → ETL → parser → Event Viewer/WEF/SIEM)
- Detecting specific behaviors via ETW providers (e.g., .NET loads, WMI activity, RPC/DCSync indicators)
- Offensive monitoring/recon without WMI and without dropping binaries; potential ETW session tampering

Keywords:
ETW, Performance Logs and Alerts, PLA, pla.dll, COM, DCOM, IDataCollectorSet, IDataCollectorSetCollection, IDataCollectorCollection, logman, perfmon, Data Collector Set, TraceDataCollector, CreateDataCollectorFromXml, Commit, namespaces, AutoLogger, Service namespace, ETL, Microsoft-Windows-DotNETRuntime, Microsoft-Windows-WMI-Activity, Microsoft-Windows-RPC, DCSync, Mimikatz, Rubeus, SYSTEM, SMB share, Event Viewer, WEF, SIEM, JonMon-Lite, EtwInspector